{
  "_comment": [
    "Procedural surface families. render/material_library.gd turns each of these",
    "into an albedo variation, a normal map and a roughness map from one shared",
    "noise field. This file is the art direction: nothing here is balance, and",
    "nothing here can change what the simulation does.",
    "",
    "The knobs, and what each one is FOR:",
    "  size          texture edge in pixels. Cost is O(size^2 x octaves) at load,",
    "                so big surfaces the camera gets close to earn 128 and small",
    "                instanced parts do not.",
    "  lattice_x/y   noise cells per octave, per axis. EQUAL counts give isotropic",
    "                grit - soil, stone, concrete. UNEQUAL counts stretch the",
    "                noise along one axis, which is what makes asphalt look",
    "                dragged, metal look brushed and bark look grained. This is",
    "                the single most important field here.",
    "  weights       how much each octave contributes. Front-loaded gives broad",
    "                soft variation; flatter gives busy fine detail.",
    "  contrast      how far the albedo swings either side of neutral.",
    "  relief        normal-map strength. High enough to catch the light, low",
    "                enough that it does not read as crumpled foil.",
    "  roughness     the surface's actual roughness - the texture carries the",
    "                absolute value, so this is the centre of the baked range.",
    "  roughness_swing  how much it varies. This is what makes a highlight look",
    "                like it is on worn metal rather than on plastic.",
    "  panel_x/y     grooves per axis, and panel_depth how deep. Manufactured",
    "                things get seams; natural things must not, because a grid is",
    "                the one thing noise can never produce.",
    "  uv_scale      how many times the map tiles across the mesh's UVs.",
    "  normal_scale  final multiplier on the normal map, per family.",
    "  seed          shifts the field so two families with identical settings are",
    "                still different surfaces."
  ],
